<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">Android and iOS need the same checkout rules, while each team wants to keep its own screen. That is already a valid sharing boundary. A later decision to share the screen adds host, navigation, input and accessibility responsibilities.</p>

Compare those boundaries by following one feature through its owners, rather than treating all shared code as one architectural choice.

<!-- @after Paradigm B: Shared UI (Compose Multiplatform) :: visual -->
### Move the boundary deliberately

<figure class="viz"><table><caption>The location port separates product needs from OS policy</caption><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Common feature: coordinates or a domain failure</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Port: explicit result and cancellation contract</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Platform adapter: permission and callback integration</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Owner: ends the operation and releases registrations</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>The boundaries in this example are different responsibilities, not implied packet transfers.</figcaption></figure>

The weather feature needs a usable location outcome, not an Android Context or a Core Location manager. Put the product-facing outcome and failure policy at the boundary: permission denied, unavailable and an acceptable location are different results. Keep prompting, platform accuracy settings and callback registration in adapters owned by their platform integration. This is an illustrative architecture choice; its accuracy and retention requirements must come from the product.

Construction ownership and operation ownership are different. An application root can create a location adapter while a screen owns one request and cancels it when leaving. Test a late callback after that cancellation and verify the adapter releases its registration according to the underlying API. A clean package graph alone cannot establish that lifetime behavior. Keep the same feature contract when replacing the platform integration so reuse does not make permissions or cleanup invisible.

<!-- @after The Boundary Pattern: Inversion of Control :: animation -->
### A location request crosses a small seam

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">A location request crosses a small seam</span><div class="stage viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Contract and use</span><div class="node" data-el="n0" data-art="parcel" data-art-role="queue"><strong>Shared feature</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n1" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Location contract</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Platform and ownership</span><div class="node" data-el="n2" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Platform adapter</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n3" data-art="worker" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Owner</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Need", "caption": "The feature needs a location outcome to fetch weather. The domain does not need an Android Context or an Apple controller.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Needs coordinates", "v1": "Outcome contract", "v2": "Not selected yet", "v3": "Screen/flow lifetime"}}, {"label": "Construct", "caption": "The platform composition root supplies a matching adapter. Its permission and accuracy policy must be explicit.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Receives dependency", "v1": "Common types", "v2": "Platform SDK integration", "v3": "Construction belongs here"}}, {"label": "Run", "caption": "The caller starts the operation under an explicit owner. The adapter translates callbacks and failures into the agreed outcome.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Awaits outcome", "v1": "Documented semantics", "v2": "Owns resources", "v3": "Owns cancellation"}}, {"label": "Dispose", "caption": "When that owner ends, the integration must release its observation/resources according to the platform contract. Test both apps.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "No stale delivery promised", "v1": "Contract tested", "v2": "Cleanup must be verified", "v3": "Lifecycle test"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/api-guidelines-build-for-multiplatform.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Pitfalls :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Must sharing rules also share the UI?</summary><p>No. Logic-only sharing and shared UI are different product boundaries.</p></details>
<details><summary>Who owns a shared ViewModel on iOS?</summary><p>The integration must supply and retain the intended owner and clear its store at that lifetime boundary.</p></details>
<details><summary>Why avoid platform-shaped interfaces?</summary><p>They can force the other target to promise semantics its OS does not provide.</p></details>


